# config.example - what BUNKER keeps in config.cfg (for reference only)
#
# BUNKER never reads this file, and you never need to copy it.
#
# config.cfg holds BUNKER's interface settings and the failed-login counter.
# BUNKER creates it on first run, next to your vault, and writes it
# encrypted (AES-256-GCM) with a key made from details of this machine and
# your bunker.salt. A config.cfg from another computer or another vault
# therefore cannot be read, which is why it is not in the repository and is
# git-ignored. To go back to the defaults below, close BUNKER and delete
# config.cfg; it is recreated at the next start. Your vault is unaffected.
#
# The contents, decrypted, with their defaults:
#
#   {
#     "attempts": 0,            failed master-password attempts so far, shared
#                               by the desktop and mobile interfaces; reset to
#                               0 by a successful login
#     "max_attempts": 3,        failed attempts allowed; on the desktop the
#                               last one must be confirmed by typing LAST, and
#                               a wrong password then destroys the vault
#     "disable_ipv4": true,     true: the BUNKER banner does not look up and
#                               show your public IP address
#     "current_timeout": 60     auto-logout after this many seconds without
#                               input (10-3600), or 0 for no auto-logout
#   }
#
# Change these from inside BUNKER (Settings), not by editing config.cfg.
